WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.680
Why food spoils, and how to start processing safely.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:08.120
This is the video for Module 1 of Small-Scale Food Processing.

3
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:19.320
At harvest time, tomatoes, pepper, plantain and cassava arrive in huge quantities. Prices crash, and a lot of food goes bad before it is sold.

4
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:27.710
Small-scale food processing turns that cheap, perishable harvest into a product that keeps for weeks or months.

5
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.230
So you can sell when buyers are ready, and at a better price.

6
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.830
But every product has a main safety risk. Knowing the risk is what lets you control it.

7
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:49.810
Fresh food spoils for three main reasons. First, microorganisms: bacteria, moulds and yeasts feed on the moisture and nutrients in food.

8
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.280
They need water, food, warmth and time. Every method you learn takes away at least one of these.

9
00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:03.120
Second, enzymes inside the food keep working after harvest, softening and browning it.

10
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:10.110
Third, pests and physical damage, like insects, rats and bruising, open the door to both.

11
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:21.750
Water is the most important factor. Properly dried foods keep. Dried pepper should snap, not bend. Garri should flow freely. Chips should crunch, not chew.

12
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.340
If a product bends or feels leathery, there is still enough water for mould.

13
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:37.040
Heat, from roasting or frying, kills most microorganisms. But once food cools and is exposed again, only good packaging protects it.

14
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:43.540
Acid slows bacteria. Fermented garri becomes slightly sour, which helps it keep.

15
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.910
Salt and sugar both pull water away from microbes.

16
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:57.180
Garri shows the science clearly. Cassava contains natural compounds that can release cyanide, and each step has a job.

17
00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.970
Peel and wash within a day or two of harvest, because the roots rot fast.

18
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:09.620
Grate into a fine mash. This lets the natural enzymes start breaking down the cyanide compounds.

19
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:17.950
Bag, ferment and press, usually for one to three days. The liquid drains out and takes some cyanide with it.

20
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.590
Sieve the cake to remove fibre and lumps.

21
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.870
Roast in a wide pan on steady heat, stirring constantly until dry and crisp.

22
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.750
Cool fully before packing, because packing warm causes mould.

23
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:44.360
For example, Bisi pressed for only half a day and roasted on high heat. Her garri went lumpy and stale. Next time she pressed for two full days and roasted on medium heat.

24
00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:51.290
Adding value means the finished product earns more than everything you spent, not just the raw food.

25
00:02:52.540 --> 00:03:01.630
Ngozi buys 10 plantain fingers for about 2,500 naira, and needs about 1,500 naira of frying oil.

26
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.250
Gas is about 500 naira, and 20 pouches with labels about 1,000 naira.

27
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:20.920
Her batch costs about 5,500 naira, or about 275 naira a pack. At 400 naira a pack, 20 packs bring in about 8,000 naira.

28
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:28.260
That leaves about 2,500 naira for her time and profit. Worth testing with one small batch first.

29
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:41.010
Hot oil causes serious burns. If oil catches fire, turn off the gas if you can do so safely, cover the pot with a lid or fire blanket, and leave it covered until cool.

30
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.100
Keep children away from the frying area, and check gas hoses with soapy water.

31
00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:56.410
Never leave frying oil unattended. Never pour water on burning oil: it explodes into a fireball. Never check for leaks with a flame.

32
00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:05.320
And never seal pepper, garlic or vegetables in oil at home. That can grow a deadly toxin you cannot see, smell or taste.

33
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:18.790
Your space must flow one way: receive, wash, process, cool, pack, store. Food never moves back from clean to dirty. If space is tight, do raw work first, then clean the surfaces.

34
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:24.350
Wash hands with soap often, cover your hair and cuts, and do not handle food when sick.

35
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:36.840
To remember. Food spoils through microbes, enzymes and pests. Dry until it snaps or crunches. Never skip a garri step. Never pour water on an oil fire.

36
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:42.160
And start with one product, chosen for access, equipment, safety and demand.

37
00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:55.700
Now score three products you could make, from 1 to 3, on raw material, equipment, safety and demand. Map which factor each step of your process controls, and draw your workspace plan.

38
00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.990
Continue with the lessons below.
